<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Two Treatises of Government</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1689">1689</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Two Treatises of Government is the principal work of political philosophy of the English philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a>, published anonymously in 1689 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a> but likely drafted in 1680-81 during the Exclusion Crisis. The First Treatise refutes, sentence by sentence, Robert Filmer's Patriarcha, which argued for a divinely ordained, hereditary, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a>. The Second Treatise sets out a society based on natural rights and contract theory, in which government requires the consent of the governed and, under limited circumstances, the people may overthrow the ruler. The book is a key foundational text in the theory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/two-treatises-of-government.jpg" alt="Two Treatises of Government" width="330" height="506" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locke_treatises_of_government_page.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…from exile in the Dutch Republic, published in 1689 (though their title pages bear the date 1690) An Essay Concerning Human Understanding, which argued that the mind begins as “white paper” furnished by experience, and <b>Two Treatises of Government</b>, which grounded government in the consent of the governed.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>John Locke’s <b>Two Treatises of Government</b> (1689) declared that absolute monarchy “is indeed inconsistent with civil society, and so can be no form of civil-government at all”, since subjects had no common judge to appeal to between themselves and their ruler.</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>John Locke’s <b>Two Treatises of Government</b>, published in 1689 though largely written earlier, grounded government in the consent of the governed and made the legislature the supreme power of the commonwealth.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>John Locke’s <b>Two Treatises of Government</b> of 1690 distinguished legislative from executive power, and Montesquieu argued in The Spirit of the Laws of 1748 that liberty was lost if the same person or body both made the laws and executed them.</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>John Locke’s <b>Two Treatises of Government</b> defined tyranny as “the exercise of power beyond right” and declared that “wherever law ends, tyranny begins”, and the United States Declaration of Independence charged George III with seeking “an absolute Tyranny”.</blockquote><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>He took from Thomas Hobbes the state of nature and the social contract but rejected Hobbes’s absolute sovereign: in his <b>Two Treatises</b> (1690) government rests on the consent of the governed and exists to protect life, liberty and property, and the people may overthrow a tyrant.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
